WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: TED Translators Admin
Reviewer: Ivana Korom

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.087
We've always thought of mentoring

00:00:02.111 --> 00:00:04.785
as the older generation
passing down wisdom to the young,

00:00:04.809 --> 00:00:07.206
but there's a huge benefit
to flipping that around

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.334
and allowing the novice
to teach the master.

00:00:09.358 --> 00:00:11.359
[The Way We Work]

00:00:12.153 --> 00:00:14.164
[Made possible
with the support of Dropbox]

00:00:14.188 --> 00:00:16.940
The workforce currently consists
of up to five generations

00:00:16.964 --> 00:00:18.394
and it's becoming more diverse

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:21.236
across all spectrums
of underrepresented groups.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.556
But that change
is not arriving nearly as fast

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.276
in C-suites and in boardrooms.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.886
Which means there's a growing gap

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.449
between leaders and their people

00:00:28.473 --> 00:00:30.896
in regards to their
perspectives and experiences.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.556
Our organizations can fall
right through that gap

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.602
into the trap of stale
thinking, blind spots

00:00:36.626 --> 00:00:38.726
and having policies that could alienate

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.296
these underrepresented groups,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.986
not only in regards to age,

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.172
race or gender,

00:00:43.196 --> 00:00:45.056
but all different kinds of viewpoints.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.214
Reverse mentoring could be an antidote
to that tunnel vision.

00:00:48.238 --> 00:00:50.146
But making reverse mentoring work,

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.887
isn't as simple as finding a mentor

00:00:51.911 --> 00:00:53.085
and pressing go.

00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:54.648
You have to be genuinely curious

00:00:54.672 --> 00:00:56.394
about learning from that individual.

00:00:56.418 --> 00:00:58.922
And you have to be intentional
about the relationship

00:00:58.946 --> 00:01:00.335
in order to make it valuable.

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:02.286
I know this because I was asked

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.556
just a year into my career
at Virgin Atlantic

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.656
to be the first ever reverse mentor

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.786
to mentor CEO Craig Kreeger.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.016
I'd met Craig a few times

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.656
and presented to him in meetings,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.026
but this, it was a whole
different ball game.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.180
Craig had revealed
that he had no black women

00:01:16.204 --> 00:01:17.378
in his inner circle

00:01:17.402 --> 00:01:19.489
and he was keen
to understand my perspective

00:01:19.513 --> 00:01:22.335
on how to build a more inclusive
culture at Virgin Atlantic.

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:23.565
No pressure.

00:01:23.589 --> 00:01:24.787
Here's what I learned

00:01:24.811 --> 00:01:26.803
about how to make reverse mentoring work.

00:01:26.827 --> 00:01:28.842
Lesson one, make your match thoughtfully.

00:01:28.866 --> 00:01:30.255
Find someone who has a pulse

00:01:30.279 --> 00:01:32.302
on the key spokespeople
in the organization

00:01:32.326 --> 00:01:33.668
to help you make your match.

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:36.196
This doesn't have to be
someone in human resources,

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.736
just someone who knows you
and your teams well,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.466
because chemistry really matters.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:42.206
The VP of people experience chose me

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.426
because I was open to sharing my ideas

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.956
and also my enthusiasm
for leadership development.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.186
Also make sure

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.157
that your mentor isn't a direct report
or part of your team,

00:01:51.181 --> 00:01:54.045
because it will be really difficult
to elicit honest feedback

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:55.982
from someone who you also have to review

00:01:56.006 --> 00:01:57.230
at the end of the year.

00:01:57.254 --> 00:01:58.477
If you're in finance,

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:00.174
find someone creative in marketing,

00:02:00.198 --> 00:02:03.118
or if you're in engineering,
find someone in customer service.

00:02:03.142 --> 00:02:05.314
This will ensure
that you develop perspectives

00:02:05.338 --> 00:02:07.042
from outside of your immediate team

00:02:07.066 --> 00:02:09.265
and different perspectives
make better leaders.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:10.465
Lesson two,

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:12.590
to make things simple,
set some ground rules.

00:02:12.614 --> 00:02:15.337
The first meeting should be offsite
in a neutral location.

00:02:15.361 --> 00:02:17.608
If you're the mentee,
you should set the agenda.

00:02:17.632 --> 00:02:19.664
What is it that you really want to learn?

00:02:19.688 --> 00:02:21.316
Maybe you'd like to understand

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.699
your mentor's career journey

00:02:22.723 --> 00:02:25.121
or perhaps whether
they've had any major obstacles

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:26.312
they've had to overcome,

00:02:26.336 --> 00:02:27.971
or maybe you'd like to understand

00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:30.230
how specific company policies impact them

00:02:30.254 --> 00:02:32.522
either directly or indirectly.

00:02:32.546 --> 00:02:34.370
Agree that your conversations together

00:02:34.394 --> 00:02:35.592
will be confidential

00:02:35.616 --> 00:02:38.156
and whether there are any topics
which are off limits,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.370
such as family life

00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:41.119
or specific feedback on individuals.

00:02:41.143 --> 00:02:43.173
Lesson three, start with an icebreaker.

00:02:43.197 --> 00:02:44.397
I like to think of this

00:02:44.421 --> 00:02:46.572
as a long elevator pitch
of your life stories.

00:02:46.596 --> 00:02:47.772
Who are you?

00:02:47.796 --> 00:02:49.263
Poignant moments in your life.

00:02:49.287 --> 00:02:50.933
What are your hopes and dreams?

00:02:50.957 --> 00:02:53.595
Pivot to seek differences
and not similarities

00:02:53.619 --> 00:02:56.016
because that's the real power
of reverse mentoring.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.283
Craig and I found

00:02:57.307 --> 00:02:59.362
that we had formative
experiences in common.

00:02:59.386 --> 00:03:00.711
Both of us immigrants,

00:03:00.735 --> 00:03:02.916
him a second generation
growing up in the US

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.881
and myself first generation

00:03:04.905 --> 00:03:06.886
arriving in the UK from Jamaica

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:08.176
at the age of three.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.444
But from there,

00:03:09.468 --> 00:03:11.001
our stories are quite different.

00:03:11.025 --> 00:03:13.093
Lesson four, beware of role reversion.

00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:15.126
There were a few times in our conversation

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.443
where Craig slipped
into giving me career advice.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:19.865
And I had to say,
"Craig this is really interesting

00:03:19.889 --> 00:03:21.801
and I'd love to come back to this later,

00:03:21.825 --> 00:03:23.429
but in our limited time together,

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:26.133
is there anything else you'd
like to understand from me?"

00:03:26.157 --> 00:03:27.333
Now this was quite hard,

00:03:27.357 --> 00:03:29.334
but you have to remember that as a mentor

00:03:29.358 --> 00:03:31.038
for this very short period of time,

00:03:31.062 --> 00:03:33.937
your insights are actually more valuable
to the organization.

00:03:33.961 --> 00:03:36.459
Lesson five, make time for reflection.

00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:39.117
Agree the key takeaways
from each of the sessions,

00:03:39.141 --> 00:03:41.846
either at the end
or through follow-up email

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.125
and schedule your sessions

00:03:43.149 --> 00:03:45.096
to allow time between for reflection.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.916
We found that three to four weeks
provided a great rhythm.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.235
And finally,

00:03:49.259 --> 00:03:50.946
give credit where credit's due.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.106
In the traditional mentoring relationship

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.236
the mentor isn't expected
to be given credit.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.426
However, in reverse mentoring

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.886
where the mentee actually
holds a lot of the power

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.002
accurate credit really counts.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:03.766
Forward-thinking organizations

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.796
use reverse mentoring as one of the tools

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.105
to help them build
a more inclusive environment.

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:09.327
And studies have shown

00:04:09.351 --> 00:04:11.956
that when organizations
embrace reverse mentoring

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.246
members of those underrepresented groups

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.986
feel more confident
in sharing their perspectives.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.446
And when accompanied
by a comprehensive diversity

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.871
and inclusion strategy,

00:04:20.895 --> 00:04:23.566
it leads to higher retention
amongst these groups.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.189
Personally, I found

00:04:25.213 --> 00:04:27.529
that my reverse mentoring
relationship with Craig

00:04:27.553 --> 00:04:30.516
enabled me to have a sense
of ownership and leadership

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.846
in building an inclusive
culture at Virgin.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.191
And for Craig, it showed

00:04:34.215 --> 00:04:36.702
that even when you're
at the pinnacle of your career,

00:04:36.726 --> 00:04:38.382
there's still more you can learn.

